00:34:23.710 --> 00:34:26.590
Parents have been feeding the chick for the past six months,

00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:29.960
and even after it fledges, they will support it for a year.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:48.380
another year or more. Among the animals that this young bird will be taught to

00:34:48.380 --> 00:34:53.060
catch are these black and white colobus monkeys.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:04.660
Ancestral monkeys began to live in trees. It was probably to get at the food there.

00:35:05.310 --> 00:35:08.290
Their feet came to be used for grasping, not walking.

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:15.240
And their forefeet in particular developed into fine, flexible instruments for picking the treetop food.

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:19.700
With this kind of monkey, traveling almost never involves the ground.

00:35:19.700 --> 00:35:24.400
It remains in the treetops, where it finds food and shelter.

00:35:43.530 --> 00:35:48.070
The effect that the ever-widening rift had on the forests of East Africa

00:35:48.070 --> 00:35:52.820
was to split them apart, to make them islands in a savanna sea.

00:35:53.760 --> 00:35:57.090
When that happened, the monkeys in each patch were isolated.

00:35:57.980 --> 00:35:59.980
The changing landscape affected algae.

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:05.860
altitude, rainfall, and temperature, making the forest islands subtly different from

00:36:05.910 --> 00:36:06.390
each other.

00:36:07.010 --> 00:36:12.100
But enough to cause the monkeys to change too and become distinct species.

00:36:13.440 --> 00:36:19.870
Back across the rift, 500 miles west of the Aberdeers, in the Niungwe forest of Rwanda,

00:36:20.180 --> 00:36:22.940
there are also black and white colobus monkeys.

00:36:23.890 --> 00:36:28.800
These lost contact with their cousins in the east hundreds of thousands of years ago.

00:36:29.280 --> 00:36:29.980
this far.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:33.120
is lower, warmer, and denser than the Aberdeers,

00:36:33.220 --> 00:36:35.780
and the colobus here are dressed for that.

00:36:36.580 --> 00:36:38.540
They run in troops of three or four hundred,

00:36:38.900 --> 00:36:41.120
instead of the thirty or forty in the Aberdeers.

00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:44.830
Both species, though, have all-white infants.

00:36:48.660 --> 00:36:51.130
And as they travel, following behind them

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:55.220
and sharing the food they find are black and white cast hornbills.

00:37:00.990 --> 00:37:06.720
The Ruinzori Turaco is as dependent on the trees for food and housing as the monkeys are.

00:37:18.900 --> 00:37:22.040
Birds evolved from the reptiles as daytime animals.

00:37:22.720 --> 00:37:27.490
Their main sense is vision, and color has become especially significant.

00:37:28.380 --> 00:37:29.980
In the trees, food...

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:33.140
generally fruit, is selected by shape and color.

00:37:46.570 --> 00:37:49.970
The colors of the birds themselves have also become important

00:37:49.970 --> 00:37:53.420
in locating mates and in territorial displays.

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:56.680
Light refracting through the feathers

00:37:56.710 --> 00:37:59.970
produces the brilliant hue of the great blue turquoise.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:21.290
But not all of their food is found in trees.

00:38:31.530 --> 00:38:35.950
No one knows exactly what Turacos get from the green filamentous algae,

00:38:36.540 --> 00:38:39.050
but it must be important, because to get it,

00:38:39.190 --> 00:38:42.430
they abandon their defensive stronghold in the treetops

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:45.780
and risk becoming a light snack for a crocodile.

00:38:57.880 --> 00:38:58.740
Blue Turaco.

00:39:01.920 --> 00:39:08.440
blue monkey. If the birds evolved as animals of the day, the mammals, with their keen sense of

00:39:08.440 --> 00:39:15.220
smell, took over the night. Unlimited time, though, is the key to evolution, and all such rules are

00:39:15.220 --> 00:39:20.980
always broken. Some birds moved into the night, and some mammals came back to the day.

00:39:31.720 --> 00:39:37.320
Monkeys, like Loast's monkey, imitated birds in more than just their treetop existence.

00:39:38.190 --> 00:39:43.950
They also ate the same thing and needed to develop the same primary sense, vision.

00:39:45.020 --> 00:39:47.680
In fact, monkeys invaded the bird's niche,

00:39:48.140 --> 00:39:51.280
muscling in on the bargain between trees and birds,

00:39:51.700 --> 00:39:55.640
whereby trees provided food and shelter and the animals spread seeds.

00:39:56.530 --> 00:39:59.960
Monkeys and apes are practically the only mammals that can...

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:09.760
sea color as well as birds. Olive baboon in the Mara, the plains north of the Serengeti.

00:40:10.440 --> 00:40:17.530
Unlike the permanently wet forests of the Abadairs and Neungwe, here there are alternating wet and

00:40:17.530 --> 00:40:26.440
dry seasons. Sometimes the baboons feed like ordinary monkeys. Sometimes though they do what

00:40:26.440 --> 00:40:29.420
few other monkeys do, they forage on the ground.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:35.380
Their forelimbs, besides being used to grasp branches and handle things, have another function.

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:38.780
They are simple, operational, walking legs.

00:40:50.780 --> 00:40:54.300
Baboons are primates that, because they could walk on the forest floor,

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:58.120
could also move out of the forest and onto the savannas.

00:40:58.680 --> 00:40:59.940
Unlike tree-bound...

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:06.050
monkeys, they were not confined to the ancient forest islands. Baboons have prospered in the

00:41:06.050 --> 00:41:18.860
rift, crossing the barriers that other species could not. And once upon a time, there was another

00:41:18.860 --> 00:41:29.100
primate that also left the forest and ate. One day, two adults and an adolescent walked across a small

00:41:29.100 --> 00:41:29.800
mud flat.

00:41:30.020 --> 00:41:32.720
Probably on their way to a nearby lake.

00:41:34.190 --> 00:41:36.730
Soon after they passed, there was an eruption.

00:41:37.440 --> 00:41:42.390
A layer of ash covered and hardened the mud and preserved the footprints.

00:41:43.100 --> 00:41:47.540
To be uncovered more than three and a half million years later

00:41:47.540 --> 00:41:53.620
as the world's earliest evidence of a primate that walked on two feet.

00:41:56.060 --> 00:41:59.970
Baboons, and indeed forest apes, used their forepaws.

00:42:00.350 --> 00:42:08.960
their hands as feet but this ape australopithecus afarensis was different the animal itself would

00:42:09.040 --> 00:42:15.780
have been very like a modern chimpanzee except in its posture in the shape of its hips and in the

00:42:15.780 --> 00:42:23.940
fact that its hands were free to grasp things carry things pick things up and it could travel

00:42:24.030 --> 00:42:28.680
farther and for longer periods than any primate ever had

00:42:32.300 --> 00:42:43.860
Those were the famous Laetoli footprints, not far from a place that is often and perhaps appropriately called Eden, the Ngorongoro crater in Tanzania.

00:42:47.590 --> 00:42:57.580
In fact, Ngorongoro, which is like all of the rift concentrated in a 12-mile diameter bowl, did not even exist when the Laetoli footprints were made.

00:42:58.310 --> 00:42:59.970
For three and a half million years...

00:43:00.000 --> 00:43:06.610
a long time, long enough for a 15,000 foot volcano to emerge from a valley in the eastern

00:43:06.610 --> 00:43:14.220
rift and eventually to explode, collapse, and become a huge basin, the largest crater in the

00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:20.440
whole rift system. Now, because it's surrounded by walls more than a quarter of a mile high,

00:43:20.480 --> 00:43:27.620
it's like a lost world, maintaining the atmosphere of ancient Africa in much the same way that

00:43:27.620 --> 00:43:29.850
Leotoli has kept the precise

00:43:30.030 --> 00:43:41.340
records. Ngorongoro has lakes, savannah, lowland forest, mountain forest, and all the classical

00:43:41.340 --> 00:43:47.290
East African animals, which differ from the animals of the Serengeti only in the fact that

00:43:47.290 --> 00:43:53.710
the grazers don't migrate. Almost all of them, predators and prey, live out their whole lives

00:43:53.770 --> 00:43:56.230
in their 12-mile diameter bowl.

00:44:04.530 --> 00:44:06.810
Oh

00:45:40.170 --> 00:45:43.990
On the day, the little upright apes made their footprints at Laetoli.

00:45:44.410 --> 00:45:47.570
Other animals were there too, and they left their footprints.

00:45:50.080 --> 00:45:55.220
Three and a half million years ago, the feline predator and the grazing prey were well established.

00:45:56.060 --> 00:45:59.960
The same niches were there and were filled, but the cats were...

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:06.060
have been slightly larger and saber-toothed. The main pack hunters would have been hyenas,

00:46:06.480 --> 00:46:08.380
which were then as big as lions.

00:46:36.810 --> 00:46:40.510
One animal that has probably not changed very much is this.

00:46:49.420 --> 00:46:53.460
The primates, with the eyes for seeing color and the hands for picking fruit,

00:46:53.840 --> 00:46:55.320
were turning into meat-eaters.

00:46:56.020 --> 00:46:59.980
But even operating in groups and perhaps wielding sticks and throwing stones,

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:05.150
they would still not have been able to bring down the larger animals and so

00:47:05.150 --> 00:47:09.880
they probably got a lot of their meat by scavenging they might have fought with

00:47:09.880 --> 00:47:13.570
birds like these for the remains of saber-tooth kills

00:47:38.890 --> 00:47:45.670
The widening rift has not only created steady eruptions of evolution in a landscape that has changed continuously,

00:47:46.220 --> 00:47:51.000
but has also provided some of Africa's best deposits of fossils.

00:47:51.780 --> 00:47:55.880
And this is the best of all, Olduvai Gorge.

00:47:56.800 --> 00:47:59.980
This 100-yard deep ravine in Tanzania is...

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:07.160
a record of life that goes back nearly two million years and at the bottom something besides bones

00:48:07.160 --> 00:48:19.860
stones at about 1.9 million years ago a primate appeared with a brain capacity half as big again

00:48:19.860 --> 00:48:26.340
as the upright apes and what dawned in that brain was this you don't have to wait until

00:48:26.340 --> 00:48:29.890
you find a sharp stone to cut with you can sharpen

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:39.310
one yourself these are probably hand axes the first known tools the cruder tools were made by

00:48:39.310 --> 00:48:47.580
the first known member of the genus of man homo habilis handyman the more symmetrical ones about

00:48:47.580 --> 00:48:59.970
half a million years later by his successor homo erectus and then about one and a half

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:06.200
million years later still art capturing the essence of the very experience of

00:49:06.200 --> 00:49:13.490
living on walls of stone the caves of Kondoah in Tanzania painted by full

00:49:13.490 --> 00:49:20.110
fledged homo sapiens about 6,000 years ago so recent on the evolutionary scale

00:49:20.110 --> 00:49:24.160
that it's practically the last thing that happened before spaceflight

00:49:25.110 --> 00:49:28.630
conceptually it is certainly no different from art today

00:49:35.150 --> 00:49:41.270
This film, taken in 1961, of the Hadza people of the Lake Iasi region of the Eastern Rift,

00:49:41.850 --> 00:49:48.290
gives a rare, if not unique, view of the life of a community of nomadic East African hunter-gatherers.

00:49:49.510 --> 00:49:56.540
The first Homo sapiens, no different except in the most superficial details from any human being alive today,

00:49:57.220 --> 00:49:59.980
appeared in this part of the world about a hundred years ago.

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:07.120
hundred thousand years ago. The Hadza people are now living where those people lived, following a

00:50:07.120 --> 00:50:27.700
way of life that might have some resemblances to theirs. The hand that evolved to clasp tree

00:50:27.700 --> 00:50:29.740
branches and pick berries now.

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:44.820
operates a complicated hunting weapon. The enormous human brain is capable of

00:50:44.820 --> 00:50:47.510
anticipating the future, planning for it.

00:51:22.330 --> 00:51:27.650
The fingers are still used for plucking fruit, but the arms are adorned by art.

00:51:28.550 --> 00:51:29.970
The whole activity is for...

00:51:30.030 --> 00:51:33.130
by a complexity of words and grammar.

00:51:35.070 --> 00:51:37.350
Berries, instead of being eaten on the spot,

00:51:37.730 --> 00:51:42.690
are kept for a meal in that characteristically human time later.

00:51:52.910 --> 00:51:55.430
The ability to fashion tools, to reason,

00:51:55.630 --> 00:51:59.010
and to learn from both experience and previous generations

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:04.610
food that cannot initially be seen or sensed in any other way.

00:52:14.760 --> 00:52:20.500
And the old primate custom of operating in groups is expanded by the new brain into the

00:52:20.500 --> 00:52:22.960
concept of community and home.

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:35.700
So that eating is more than subsistence. It's an aesthetic experience.

00:52:45.680 --> 00:52:50.240
Another essential for subsistence, communication, expands into conversation,

00:52:51.020 --> 00:52:53.670
storytelling, singing, enjoyment.

00:52:57.450 --> 00:52:59.890
Of all the rich animals, humans became...

00:53:00.000 --> 00:53:06.600
the most adept at adjusting mentally rather than changing physically. Whatever facet of the rift

00:53:06.600 --> 00:53:13.200
and the planet that humans have found themselves in, the formula for existence has worked. Upright

00:53:13.200 --> 00:53:20.130
posture, good vision, big brain, and free hands have led to tool making, thought, language, society,

00:53:20.620 --> 00:53:28.520
and a capacity for enjoyment. All of the details that in their variation become culture. On the

